  // ---------- UUTISET (NEWS-osio) ----------
  // Tyhja juttulista piilottaa koko osion. Jutun teksti: tyhja rivi = uusi
  // kappale, "## " = valiotsikko, "- " = luettelon kohta.
  uutiset: {
    otsikko: "NEWS",
    otsikkoSiirto: 90,
    linkkiTeksti: "",
    linkki: "",
    lueTeksti: "READ THE STORY ›",
    maara: 3,
    jutut: [
      { pvm: "AUG 22, 2026", otsikko: "Spirit Makes a Statement in Seinäjoki",
        ingressi: "7–6 thriller against S-Kiekko U20 decided with just 21 seconds remaining.",
        kuva: "",
        teksti: "Spirit of Hockey delivered one of the biggest performances of its preseason in Seinäjoki, beating S-Kiekko U20 7–6 in a game decided with just 21 seconds left on the clock.\n\nAnd this was no ordinary preseason opponent. Last season S-Kiekko finished third in the U20 Suomi-sarja with 27 points — one point behind Peliitat and two behind league winner Laser HT. Only the top two teams advanced to the U20 Mestis qualification series, which made S-Kiekko the highest-ranked team to miss the qualifiers.\n\nThat puts Spirit's victory into perspective. This was not simply seven goals in a preseason game. It was a statement that Spirit can compete with established Finnish teams, that this international group is beginning to become a real team, and that Spirit is not afraid of difficult opponents.\n\n## A difficult beginning\n\nS-Kiekko showed immediately why it was one of the strongest teams in the league last season. Lenni Korkiavuori opened the scoring after 4:07, and late in the opening period Eelis Sikkilä made it 2–0. After twenty minutes Spirit had been challenged — and now the team had to respond.\n\n## Kuismanen starts the comeback\n\nAt 24:15, while Spirit was shorthanded, Nooa Kuismanen scored to cut the deficit to 2–1. It was the beginning of an extraordinary night for him, and the beginning of Spirit's comeback.\n\nAt 30:34 Alexander Ladouceur tied the game from a Kuismanen assist, and at 33:48 Eelis Yrjänäinen scored on the power play — assisted by Spirit goaltender Miska Podschivalow. From 0–2 down, Spirit led 3–2.\n\n## Spirit takes control, and loses it\n\nOne minute into the third period Ladouceur struck again, this time assisted by Endika Fernandez and, remarkably, Podschivalow again. 4–2, and four unanswered Spirit goals.\n\nBut S-Kiekko was never going to disappear. Twelve seconds later Akseli Paaso made it 3–4, and 37 seconds after that Vili Viljanen tied the game. At 48:12 Korkiavuori scored on the power play and the home team led 5–4. Spirit had gone from two goals behind to two goals ahead — and back behind again.\n\n## Lapham and Kuismanen answer\n\nAt 53:14 Easton Lapham delivered the equaliser from assists by Yrjänäinen and Kuismanen. At 55:22 Kuismanen scored his second of the night and Spirit led 6–5.\n\nThere was still one more twist. Spirit took a penalty inside the final four minutes and Viljanen scored again at 57:02. With less than three minutes remaining the game was tied at 6–6. Many teams would have accepted the draw. Spirit didn't.\n\n## 59:39\n\nWith twenty-one seconds remaining, Miro Hase and Easton Lapham created one final chance — and Nooa Kuismanen finished it. 7–6. A hat trick completed, another comeback completed, and this time there was no answer.\n\n## Kuismanen: 3+2 and the game winner\n\n- 3 goals and 2 assists\n- one shorthanded goal\n- the game-winning goal at 59:39\n\nHis first goal started the comeback from 0–2. He assisted Ladouceur's 2–2 and Lapham's 5–5. He scored to make it 6–5. And with 21 seconds left he completed his hat trick and won the game — against one of last season's strongest U20 Suomi-sarja teams.\n\n## Podschivalow: 46 saves and two assists\n\nAt the other end Spirit needed another exceptional performance. Miska Podschivalow faced 52 shots — 15 in the first period, 19 in the second, 18 in the third — and stopped 46 of them.\n\nStopping pucks wasn't his only contribution: he was credited with assists on both Yrjänäinen's 3–2 goal and Ladouceur's 4–2 goal. Forty-six saves, two assists, one victory. That is a performance to remember.\n\n## Contributions throughout the lineup\n\nThis was not a one-player victory. Ladouceur scored twice, including the goal that gave Spirit a 4–2 lead. Yrjänäinen produced a goal and an assist. Lapham scored the crucial 5–5 equaliser and assisted the winner. Fernandez picked up an assist on Ladouceur's second goal, and Hase assisted the game winner.\n\nSpirit also found different ways to score: seven goals, one shorthanded, two on the power play — and when the biggest moment arrived, the winner came at even strength with 21 seconds remaining.\n\n## Respect for the opponent\n\nGreat victories require great opponents, and S-Kiekko's results last season speak for themselves. The Seinäjoki team finished third in the U20 Suomi-sarja, one point away from the U20 Mestis qualification series.\n\nThat is exactly why games like this matter for Spirit. We want difficult opponents. We want teams that punish mistakes. We want games that demand discipline, courage and resilience, and situations where players have to find another level. Development does not come from choosing the easy road.\n\n## More than a preseason victory\n\nNobody wins a season in August, and there is still a huge amount of work ahead. This team is still being built. Players have arrived in Finland from different countries, different hockey cultures and different backgrounds, and they are still learning each other's habits, the system, and how to win together.\n\nBut there are moments in the development of a team when one game tells you something important. This was one of those moments. Spirit went 0–2 down. Spirit fought back to lead 4–2 and lost that lead. Spirit fell behind 5–4 and fought back again. S-Kiekko tied the game with less than three minutes remaining. And Spirit still found a way to win.\n\n## An international group becoming a team\n\nPlayers from Finland, Canada, the United States, Spain and other countries have come together with one goal: to develop, compete and become better hockey players. Different languages, different backgrounds, different styles of hockey — one locker room, one team.\n\nIn Seinäjoki, when the game became difficult, Spirit didn't break apart. It came together. That may ultimately be the most important result of the night.\n\n## Final score\n\n- S-Kiekko U20 6 — Spirit of Hockey U20 7\n\n## Spirit scoring\n\n- Nooa Kuismanen — 3+2, game-winning goal at 59:39\n- Alexander Ladouceur — 2+0\n- Eelis Yrjänäinen — 1+1\n- Easton Lapham — 1+1\n- Miska Podschivalow — 0+2, 46 saves\n- Endika Fernandez — 0+1\n- Miro Hase — 0+1\n\nOne game does not define a season. But some games show what a team can become. We are Spirit. We work. We learn. We compete. We fight together. The journey continues." }
    ]
  },

